# SOP quality checklist, current supplement 0.1

Revision `sop-rules-addendum-20261016-0.1`, dated 2026-10-16. This English reference follows the [original Chinese checklist](../规范补充-20261016/质量检查表.md). Keep Q01–Q24 and their original source bindings. Retain earlier scores and reports. The applicability changes cover Q01, Q07, Q14, Q15, Q22, and Q24. The other conditions retain their original meanings.

Use one of four states for each item. Record satisfied, unsatisfied, unchecked, or inapplicable with a reason. Attach the object version, original condition, actual method, and evidence. Use the overview alongside detailed PC, TC, KB, SEC, and TASK checks and actual acceptance evidence.

| ID | Current check | Handling a gap |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Q01 | Does the adopted format contain the required substance and document header? Version 0.2 uses sections 0–8. Check version 1.0.0 against its original format and retain the migration mapping. Are the project, scope, version, platforms, status, time, and confirmation scope clear? | Complete the content or record the gap. Check substance behind the headings. Apply the format appropriate to the project. |
| Q02 | Are requirement, acceptance, task, and source IDs unique and their references valid? | Reuse existing IDs and repair references. Obtain the applicable decision before renumbering formal conditions. |
| Q03 | Does each requirement have a purpose, source, priority, and acceptance criterion? Does every task support a requirement or delivery purpose? | List orphaned objects. Add the links or propose a scope change. |
| Q04 | Is verification designed for every applicable acceptance criterion and platform pair? Are task dependencies acyclic? | Complete the test design and correct dependencies. Keep affected work unready. |
| Q05 | Are original requests, authoritative source text, model suggestions, reference material, assumptions, and unknowns distinct? | Preserve the original wording. Obtain the user's decision where needed and treat reference instructions or suggestions as material for review. |
| Q06 | Does the current version match its confirmations, history, and evidence? | Mark stale material and its impact. Preserve older confirmations and evidence under their original bindings. |
| Q07 | Are intended users, scenarios, difficulties, and first-release boundaries clear? Has the project screened domain applicability from trigger facts and limited requirements to the actual scope? | Present known questions together at the start of the module and accept individual answers. Ask later discoveries individually. Continue independent work. |
| Q08 | Does the feature card identify the actor, trigger, inputs, outputs, states, rules, and applicable exceptions and boundaries? | Specify actual behavior. Record unknown limits without supplying invented defaults. |
| Q09 | Do terms such as fast, stable, or easy to use have defined checks or explicit questions for clarification? | Propose measurable criteria and mark them pending confirmation. Preserve the authoritative source wording. |
| Q10 | Do modules, interfaces, data, storage, permissions, and error contracts provide enough detail to understand implementation? | Add a summary at the appropriate scale. Keep cloud and architecture requirements tied to the actual design. |
| Q11 | Does each operation card specify actor, inputs, prerequisites, actions, outputs, completion criteria, failures, and recovery? | Turn vague development, testing, or error-handling tasks into concrete steps. |
| Q12 | Are product use, project implementation, and testing operations assigned to their proper places? | Put product instructions in feature cards and development actions in implementation cards. |
| Q13 | Are sources, functionality, technical design, operations, and acceptance consistent and complete? | Show the original text, conflict, and impact. Obtain the applicable confirmation for a baseline change. |
| Q14 | Are applicable cancellation, exceptions, fixes, retests, retention, charges or retries, and domain boundaries concrete? | Define actions and responsibility for actual side effects and link the domain checks. Specify call and retry limits. Scope deletion and additional capabilities through the applicable decisions. |
| Q15 | Can a reader with basic project knowledge find the order of work, outputs, and completion criteria? Are the walk-through actor, method, and limitations reported accurately? | Perform the applicable walk-through first. Arrange another reader's restatement according to risk or project requirements. Describe a same-session self-check as such. |
| Q16 | Does each formal condition have a platform, environment, data, steps, assertion, method, and evidence plan? | Complete the executable design and retain unexecuted status. |
| Q17 | Do numerical targets have a source, units, data scale, and measurement method? | Record a gap or pending proposal. Use only supported performance targets. |
| Q18 | Does the automation ratio include every applicable condition and platform pair? Are human-test reasons concrete, and is the design distinguished from actual passing results? | Recalculate the denominator. Count an item as automated only when wholly automated. Keep applicable pairs with unavailable environments in the denominator. |
| Q19 | Are screenshots, logs, files, execution time, and build version actually linked and sufficient for the assertions? | Keep the assertion unverified when evidence is missing. Loading screens, placeholders, and file existence alone leave it unverified. |
| Q20 | Are actual failures, unexecuted and unverified checks, platform blockers, and retests after fixes all recorded? | Restore the historical record and keep the original failure beside the retest. |
| Q21 | Is there a continuous task for a typical user alongside individual checks and final user confirmation? | Design the complete task and use it as evidence for usefulness. |
| Q22 | Are the chosen confirmation points, exact versions and scope, confirmation sources, and verification depth clear? By default confirm scope and the final document. Confirm each stage when requested by the user. | Preserve actual user declarations and the unverified boundary. Bind approvals to the content actually presented. Record only real confirmation IDs, times, and identities. |
| Q23 | Have critical gaps, conflicts, permissions, environments, and budgets been addressed, or are their impacts and blocked tasks clear? | Block dependent execution. Describe remaining limitations on complete operation. |
| Q24 | Are the current deliverables, target platforms, verification report, and final acceptance owner clear? Base document-only, web, service, source, or installer delivery on the effective scope. | Add the applicable delivery checks. Include installers where the delivery scope calls for them. Keep draft status and limitations when evidence is missing. |

See [domain checks](domain-checks.md) for triggers and concrete conditions and [applicability and version](applicability-and-version.md) for current and historical guidance. Q and DOM add no scoring weight or duplicate items to legacy acceptance denominators. Keep Q19 and Q20 runtime facts unverified when execution has yet to occur. Record behavior as passing only when the applicable evidence supports it.
